finding: Stage 8 does not preserve the confirmation stream's semantics and does not yet cover the spike's recovery case. Findings: 1. src/extras/multiplayer/index.ts:430-445 and prediction.ts:208,199,423-424: flush("latest") throws away all earlier packets' intents and confirmation observations, not merely their replays. It also feeds ClientInterpolation only the final pose. Independent composed-world probe queued ticks 1,2,3, with an accepted operation only in tick 1: after step(0), prediction.records was empty, interpolation.confirm received only [3], and confirmed observation received only [3]. record(tick, intents) does not require every later packet to repeat earlier intents. Skipped accepted operations and confirmation-only one-off outputs can be lost permanently; a remote body's intermediate path/turns are lost even when all packets arrived. Preserve per-confirmation acceptance/observations and presentation samples while coalescing expensive replay. Existing pacing test has an empty applyIntent, samples only its predicted local body, and cannot detect these losses. 2. src/extras/multiplayer/prediction.ts:235-248: lead is a lifetime high-water mark, with no downward feedback path. Independent probe raised 2 to 23 with one lateness=20 record; 98 later fresh zero-lateness records left it at 23. After a transient spike, the extra future scheduling/authoritative latency persists for the session, potentially at the lead cap. No measured need earns this divergence from bidirectional time sync; the test asserts growth and a loose maximum, not relaxation after recovery. 3. src/extras/multiplayer/prediction.ts:335-359: far-behind confirmation/image handling is still unbounded explicit fixed work inside preFixed, not a bounded image fast-forward. A fresh image at tick 401 delivered to a tick-1 client with default maxAhead=32 caused 400 full fixed callbacks in one step(0), before applying the image. The updated confirmed tick makes the bound permit all of them. This predecessor-path limitation remains material to stage 8's 2-vCPU / hundreds-behind premise: replay-once does not bound the one catch-up run. Existing test only checks fixed-work bounds before frame 1900 and tests a 50-half-tick stall, not hundreds-behind image recovery. A 20%-slow clock itself can converge (0.8*1.5=1.2), but that does not prove bounded catch-up work on the slow host. 4. src/extras/multiplayer/prediction.ts:219-221: pacing replaces rather than composes with game time scale. With ClientPrediction installed, setting game scale to 0 then stepping changed scale to 0.06 on the first frame in an independent probe; later steps resume simulation. Slow motion is likewise driven back toward the controller's 0.8-1.5 range. Ownership is documented, but there is no independent game-time control/composition seam. World.pause() has a separate flag; this finding concerns pause/slow motion via the public setTimeScale API, not that flag. Independently examined: commit diff; prediction, receiver, interpolation and pacing/related tests; requested unit stages 8-10, witnessed notes, referent entries, CONTRIBUTING Runtime frame schedule / Code / Tests. Temporary composed-world probes were removed and mutations restored; HEAD remains 77a3aaa. Checks: bun test ./src/extras/multiplayer/*.test.ts ./src/extras/multiplayer/*.node.ts: 37 pass, including pacing and allocation fixtures. bun test --todo ./scripts/check-*.node.ts: 301 pass. Independent probes confirmed the behaviors above. Mutation controls independently confirmed the pacing test rejects per-message reconciliation (5 confirmations) and fixed lead (input 3 ticks late). Notes/uncertainty: the drawn-motion test really reads readRenderGlobalTransform. At 60 units/s and roughly half a tick/frame, the 0.08 increment-spread check is meaningful against integer-tick stepping; the positive per-frame advance and <0.76 jump checks also reject gross freezes/jumps during its short burst, and long-hold alpha is checked. These do not exercise a remote body's lost intermediate confirmations or general trajectories. No independent physical-display or 2-vCPU-host run was made; no claim about measured wall-clock recovery latency. Full pre-land sweep not run (review only).
